\section{Introduction}
Wara (ISO 639-3: wbf; Glottocode wara1292) is spoken in southwestern Burkina Faso, in villages near the Mali border in the Cascades region (Leraba province, around Sindou). Joshua Project reports a population of about 11,000. Other names include Ouala, Ouara, Samoe and Samue. Global Recordings Network lists varieties including Soulani, Faniagara, Negueni-Klani and Ouatourou-Niasogoni. The searches found no school materials, so this report is short.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Wara turned up in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia entry on the Wara language, which says the name can refer to either of two Gur languages of Burkina Faso, so check it against the identification above: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wara_language}
\item Joshua Project language page for wbf: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/index.php/languages/wbf}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project people-group profile, which reports that Bible translation has started and that no audio Bible or Jesus Film exists: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/15862}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network, Wara language page (evangelistic and Bible-teaching audio): \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/wbf}
\item Global Recordings Network, Soulani variety: \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/18193}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The searches found no orthography, dictionary, grammar or school material for Wara. A curriculum would have to start from basic documentation and community work. The only audio found is religious.
